WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.906
This robot can put a bowl on a cabinet.

2
00:00:03.016 --> 00:00:04.404
It can open a drawer.

3
00:00:04.525 --> 00:00:08.557
Ask for both in one job, and it gets it
right in just 1 of 20 fresh

4
00:00:08.577 --> 00:00:10.267
tries.

5
00:00:14.717 --> 00:00:17.301
Robot AI models are learning skills fast.

6
00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:20.130
But a business needs whole
jobs done reliably,

7
00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.512
in its own setting, and its
jobs keep changing.

8
00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:26.577
Turning skills into reliable
work is engineering:

9
00:00:26.722 --> 00:00:29.198
watch the failures, find the cause,

10
00:00:29.218 --> 00:00:31.117
change the system, prove it.

11
00:00:31.227 --> 00:00:33.192
Today, specialists do that by hand,

12
00:00:33.212 --> 00:00:38.280
for every change, and that makes
automation slow and expensive.

13
00:00:39.217 --> 00:00:42.448
Mayfly is building robot installations
whose engineering is done

14
00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:43.867
by AI agents.

15
00:00:44.209 --> 00:00:46.209
Before building that on real robots,

16
00:00:46.229 --> 00:00:48.674
we tested the core question in simulation:

17
00:00:48.935 --> 00:00:52.157
can an agent find out on its own
what needs to change,

18
00:00:52.232 --> 00:00:54.316
change it, and prove that it works?

19
00:00:54.693 --> 00:00:58.412
We ran two experiments, one for each kind
of engineering an installation

20
00:00:58.432 --> 00:00:59.355
keeps needing.

21
00:00:59.697 --> 00:01:03.674
The first: a new job, made from
skills the robot already has.

22
00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:07.436
Every new product or packing rule
asks for a job like this,

23
00:01:07.511 --> 00:01:10.199
and today a specialist has to
make it reliable.

24
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.472
The second: faults we planted in
a fleet of robots.

25
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.603
When robots that worked
yesterday start to fail,

26
00:01:17.623 --> 00:01:20.799
someone has to find out why,
and repair it.

27
00:01:23.217 --> 00:01:26.494
The test bench is a physics simulator
with a kitchen scene from

28
00:01:26.514 --> 00:01:28.274
a public robotics benchmark.

29
00:01:28.407 --> 00:01:32.659
In simulation, every start can be
repeated exactly and every motion

30
00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:36.331
measured, and the kitchen makes the
failures easy to follow.

31
00:01:36.673 --> 00:01:42.345
The robot runs X-VLA, an open robot
vision-language-action model:

32
00:01:42.478 --> 00:01:45.955
it turns camera images and a written
instruction into motion.

33
00:01:46.077 --> 00:01:48.463
For these experiments we kept it fixed,

34
00:01:48.538 --> 00:01:51.935
so every gain comes from the
engineering around it.

35
00:01:52.383 --> 00:01:55.547
The engineer is an AI agent in a sandbox.

36
00:01:55.773 --> 00:01:57.936
Like a robotics engineer at a desk,

37
00:01:57.956 --> 00:02:01.047
it looks at camera frames from its trials
and reads the measurements

38
00:02:01.067 --> 00:02:03.953
the simulator records: where the hand is,

39
00:02:03.982 --> 00:02:06.995
where each object is, what touches what.

40
00:02:07.174 --> 00:02:10.869
It writes its own checks, builds
programs from the robot's skills

41
00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:12.254
and runs experiments.

42
00:02:12.538 --> 00:02:14.318
It knows the rules it is graded by,

43
00:02:14.338 --> 00:02:15.749
and cannot change them.

44
00:02:15.917 --> 00:02:18.350
The faults we planted stay hidden from it,

45
00:02:18.378 --> 00:02:22.192
and the exam layouts stay locked away
until its solution is final.

46
00:02:22.302 --> 00:02:25.594
We built the bench, wrote the
requests and fixed the grading.

47
00:02:25.751 --> 00:02:29.713
After that, no person took part: the
agent chose every experiment

48
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:31.875
and wrote every solution.

49
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:37.911
Each try starts from a stored
arrangement of the table:

50
00:02:37.987 --> 00:02:41.693
a layout. The agent compares its
programs on the same practice

51
00:02:41.713 --> 00:02:46.047
layouts. 20 more stay locked away for a
final exam it never sees

52
00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:50.029
in advance. On those same 20, we
also run the plain chain,

53
00:02:50.049 --> 00:02:52.354
the skills simply one after the other.

54
00:02:52.453 --> 00:02:53.736
That is the baseline.

55
00:02:53.881 --> 00:02:57.518
Later, on real robots, the practice
layouts will be simulated copies

56
00:02:57.538 --> 00:03:00.594
of the real station, and the final
exam will take place in the

57
00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:01.491
real world.

58
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:05.293
Experiment one.

59
00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:07.917
The request, as a shift lead
would write it:

60
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.165
bowl on top of the cabinet,
middle drawer open,

61
00:03:11.185 --> 00:03:13.443
everything at rest, hand clear.

62
00:03:13.576 --> 00:03:17.457
The bar: the whole job completed
without a failure on at least

63
00:03:17.477 --> 00:03:20.096
18 of 20 fresh layouts.

64
00:03:22.517 --> 00:03:24.822
Chained plainly, the job falls apart.

65
00:03:24.955 --> 00:03:27.899
The drawer skill starts the moment
the bowl counts as placed.

66
00:03:27.997 --> 00:03:29.591
But the hand still holds the bowl,

67
00:03:29.611 --> 00:03:31.173
and drags it off.

68
00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:37.534
The agent studies its first runs and
writes a check for exactly

69
00:03:37.554 --> 00:03:38.547
this hand-off.

70
00:03:38.633 --> 00:03:42.421
In all 10, the next skill began while
the bowl was still touched

71
00:03:42.441 --> 00:03:46.125
and moving. The placing skill reports
success before the job is

72
00:03:46.145 --> 00:03:47.185
done.

73
00:03:48.617 --> 00:03:50.864
Then it changes one thing at a time,

74
00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:52.930
on the same practice layouts.

75
00:03:53.215 --> 00:03:54.695
Open the fingers first?

76
00:03:54.886 --> 00:03:56.413
They still catch the bowl.

77
00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:57.725
Go home first?

78
00:03:57.951 --> 00:03:59.571
The way home snags it.

79
00:03:59.658 --> 00:04:02.354
What works: let the placement finish,

80
00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:05.527
so the hand lets go and
lifts, then go home,

81
00:04:05.602 --> 00:04:09.393
then open the drawer: 10 of 10
successful jobs.

82
00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.641
Before the exam, it confirms the
program on 20 practice layouts

83
00:04:14.661 --> 00:04:15.816
it had never used.

84
00:04:16.402 --> 00:04:21.285
20 of 20. Then the final exam, on
the 20 locked layouts.

85
00:04:21.975 --> 00:04:23.862
The plain chain gets 1 right.

86
00:04:24.506 --> 00:04:28.053
The agent's job: 20 of 20.

87
00:04:33.483 --> 00:04:36.760
The job and its check go into the
installation's library,

88
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.412
ready for the next request.

89
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:43.021
From request to proven job, the
agent took 41 minutes.

90
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.907
The second request is harder.

91
00:04:46.214 --> 00:04:49.526
Two things must end up in place: the
plate at the front of the

92
00:04:49.546 --> 00:04:52.083
stove, and the bowl on top of the cabinet.

93
00:04:52.425 --> 00:04:56.179
The robot has a skill for each:
one pushes the plate,

94
00:04:56.199 --> 00:04:57.726
the other places the bowl.

95
00:04:58.056 --> 00:05:02.857
Chained plainly, the job succeeds on
5 of 20 fresh layouts.

96
00:05:03.176 --> 00:05:06.294
The trouble is a pack of cream
cheese in the plate's way:

97
00:05:06.427 --> 00:05:10.485
the push runs into it, or the hand
grabs the cheese instead.

98
00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:15.056
Starting from the check it saved
in the first job,

99
00:05:15.076 --> 00:05:17.334
the agent tries 18 programs.

100
00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:19.862
Loading the cheese into the
bowl clears the way,

101
00:05:19.882 --> 00:05:21.688
and every plate push works.

102
00:05:21.926 --> 00:05:24.079
But now the loaded bowl won't carry:

103
00:05:24.154 --> 00:05:26.459
the job delivers 4 of 10.

104
00:05:26.546 --> 00:05:29.246
Fixing one problem created the next.

105
00:05:30.683 --> 00:05:33.801
Its answer: pick up the cheese
and set it aside.

106
00:05:33.957 --> 00:05:36.181
Then the plate, then the empty bowl:

107
00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:39.498
10 of 10. On the final exam, 20 of 20,

108
00:05:39.518 --> 00:05:42.032
against 5 for the plain chain.

109
00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:46.627
What did the first experiment show?

110
00:05:46.865 --> 00:05:50.749
Twice, a new job failed when the robot's
skills were simply chained.

111
00:05:50.928 --> 00:05:53.454
Twice, the agent found the physical cause,

112
00:05:53.529 --> 00:05:56.865
changed how the job is done, and
proved its solution on layouts

113
00:05:56.885 --> 00:05:59.886
it had never seen, with no
person in the loop.

114
00:05:59.996 --> 00:06:03.517
That matters, because this is the
engineering that makes every

115
00:06:03.537 --> 00:06:05.679
new job slow and expensive today.

116
00:06:05.824 --> 00:06:09.824
Here, an agent did it, in 41
and 113 minutes.

117
00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:15.392
Experiment two asks a different question:

118
00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:17.200
when working robots start to fail,

119
00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:18.898
can the agent find out why?

120
00:06:19.019 --> 00:06:22.239
We built a fleet of 12
simulated arms at 3 sites,

121
00:06:22.259 --> 00:06:25.400
doing pick-and-place work, and
planted problems in it.

122
00:06:25.521 --> 00:06:27.432
The agent is told nothing about them.

123
00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.819
It sees what an operator sees: results,

124
00:06:30.839 --> 00:06:33.570
telemetry, change and maintenance logs,

125
00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:36.360
and the actions an operator can take.

126
00:06:37.283 --> 00:06:38.590
Here is what we planted.

127
00:06:38.688 --> 00:06:41.814
At the first site, the model that
drives the robots runs in the

128
00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:45.614
customer's distant data centre, and
the line adds a delay.

129
00:06:45.712 --> 00:06:48.597
The customer requires that
link, so it stays.

130
00:06:48.719 --> 00:06:52.170
At the second site, one robot's
controller is overloaded and falls

131
00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.990
behind. At the third site, a software
update crops part of every

132
00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.245
camera image before the model sees it.

133
00:06:58.459 --> 00:07:01.945
On the same day, a camera's
calibration record is rewritten,

134
00:07:01.965 --> 00:07:03.782
which changes nothing at all.

135
00:07:03.904 --> 00:07:06.000
That one is our false lead.

136
00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:11.092
The agent is not told what
causes the failures.

137
00:07:11.248 --> 00:07:13.228
It sees the operating records.

138
00:07:13.373 --> 00:07:16.603
The robots at the first site trail
their commands and succeed far

139
00:07:16.623 --> 00:07:20.507
less often. One robot at the second
site trails just the same.

140
00:07:20.641 --> 00:07:24.292
The robots at the third site fail more
often than the healthy robots,

141
00:07:24.425 --> 00:07:27.613
and the change log shows two
changes on the same day.

142
00:07:27.711 --> 00:07:29.552
Same symptom, same cause?

143
00:07:29.685 --> 00:07:32.477
And which of the two changes is to blame?

144
00:07:34.417 --> 00:07:37.210
The task records can't tell the
first two apart.

145
00:07:37.389 --> 00:07:39.311
So the agent builds an instrument:

146
00:07:39.363 --> 00:07:42.942
a small test motion, with the
robot's model switched off,

147
00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.337
timing how each arm follows a command.

148
00:07:45.424 --> 00:07:48.181
At the first site, the hand
always starts late,

149
00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.003
by the same amount: a delay on the line.

150
00:07:51.089 --> 00:07:54.613
The robot at the second site
starts nearly on time,

151
00:07:54.805 --> 00:07:57.330
then falls further and further behind:

152
00:07:57.463 --> 00:07:59.373
its controller can't keep up.

153
00:07:59.472 --> 00:08:02.064
They look alike, but they are two
different problems,

154
00:08:02.084 --> 00:08:04.436
and they need different repairs.

155
00:08:06.383 --> 00:08:09.210
Each repair is tested on its own
before it is adopted.

156
00:08:09.448 --> 00:08:11.611
The first site's link has to stay,

157
00:08:11.631 --> 00:08:14.699
so the agent has the robots skip
commands that would be out of

158
00:08:14.719 --> 00:08:16.211
date by the time they arrive.

159
00:08:16.426 --> 00:08:19.404
It tests three compensation
settings and keeps the best.

160
00:08:19.514 --> 00:08:22.492
The overloaded controller at the
second site is serviced.

161
00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:26.077
At the third site, it undoes each of the
two changes in a separate

162
00:08:26.097 --> 00:08:29.923
test. Rolling back the software
update brings the site back.

163
00:08:30.056 --> 00:08:32.918
Rolling back the calibration
shows no clear effect.

164
00:08:33.005 --> 00:08:35.356
So it adopts the software rollback,

165
00:08:35.385 --> 00:08:37.748
and leaves the calibration alone.

166
00:08:40.183 --> 00:08:44.903
Then the test that counts: 576
attempts the agent never saw.

167
00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:48.305
As the fleet was, 451 successes.

168
00:08:48.391 --> 00:08:51.764
With the agent's 3 changes, 558.

169
00:08:51.909 --> 00:08:54.655
From 78% to 97%.

170
00:08:54.777 --> 00:08:58.022
Against what we planted: all
three problems found,

171
00:08:58.051 --> 00:09:01.888
each with a tested repair, and the
false lead left alone.

172
00:09:04.817 --> 00:09:08.562
In both experiments, the agent did
the engineering itself.

173
00:09:08.660 --> 00:09:13.391
Two new jobs: from 1 to 20 of
20, and from 5 to 20.

174
00:09:13.478 --> 00:09:17.501
A fleet: from 78% to 97%.

175
00:09:17.681 --> 00:09:21.179
The robots' vision-language-action
model and their overall design

176
00:09:21.199 --> 00:09:23.086
stayed the same throughout.

177
00:09:24.517 --> 00:09:27.937
What we learned: the agent worked
like a good engineer.

178
00:09:28.023 --> 00:09:31.152
It looked at the evidence, found
the physical cause,

179
00:09:31.181 --> 00:09:34.714
changed one thing at a time, built a
measurement when the records

180
00:09:34.734 --> 00:09:38.034
weren't enough, and proved its
solutions on cases it had never

181
00:09:38.054 --> 00:09:40.960
seen. These were first, compact tests,

182
00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:44.060
and in each one the agent carried the
investigation through to

183
00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.943
a working result.

184
00:09:48.383 --> 00:09:50.851
Next, we take this to real robots:

185
00:09:50.902 --> 00:09:54.589
a complete packing operation on
two-armed mobile robots,

186
00:09:54.618 --> 00:09:58.571
where agents engineer new work on the
hardware and prove it there.

187
00:09:58.844 --> 00:10:02.620
The question we will answer: how much
less human work each new

188
00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.048
job needs, once agents do the engineering.

189
00:10:07.483 --> 00:10:11.027
That is what Mayfly is building: robot
installations where taking

190
00:10:11.047 --> 00:10:12.989
on new work is part of the product,

191
00:10:13.009 --> 00:10:15.117
because agents do the engineering.
